course: dictionary
lesson: 4761
title: Household: the home and its people
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A household is all the people who live together in one home. Think of your family under one roof. «Council tax is paid by each household to the local council,» so each home pays once, not each person.
explain: As an adjective, household means used in or about the home. «A chore chart lists the household jobs and who does each one.» Household jobs are the jobs done around the house, like washing up or tidying.
explain: A household name is different. It is someone or something that almost everyone knows. The word has a clue: it means a name that is known in every home.
fact: A household is all the people who live together in one home.
fact: Household is also an adjective, so it can describe things: household chores, household items and household goods are all things used in or about the home.
fact: A household name is someone or something that almost everyone knows.
fact: Household chemicals, like bleach and cleaners, must be kept away from small children.
fact: Households share chores and bills, so the work and the costs are shared between the people who live there.
exercise: «A chore chart lists the household jobs and who does each one.» What are household jobs? | Jobs done in a shop | Jobs done at school by teachers | Jobs done on a farm only | *Jobs done around the home, like washing up
exercise: Ella says a household name is a name for a house. What does household name really mean? | A name for a family pet | A name written on a front door | *Someone or something almost everyone knows | A name for a new street
exercise: «Council tax is paid by each household to the local council.» Find and copy the word that tells who pays the tax. | *household | council | parks | libraries
test: «African grey parrots copy household noises.» What are household noises? | *Sounds made around the home, like a kettle or a door | Sounds heard only in churches | Sounds from a football match | Sounds of ships out at sea
test: «Households share chores and bills.» Who shares the chores? | *The people who live together in one home | Only the head teacher | Only the shopkeeper | Only the council
exam: «A household is all the people who live together in one home.» Which meaning of household fits this sentence best? | *All the people who live together in one home | A bill paid to the council | Used in or about the home, as an adjective only | A name almost everyone knows
